In June 2000, Samsung announced that it could produce 1.2 GHz Alphas.
There was a deafening silence from Compaq.  Early this year, either
or both of IBM and Samsung made it clear that they could deliver
1 GHz Alphas in quantity.  Compaq repeated their silence.

The DS10/DS20/ES40 boards were already inadequate for the 667 MHz
Alphas, both because of memory bandwidth and because of their 33 MHz
PCI bus.  The latter was particularly painful for HPC customers,
because they could get a faster network (Quadrics) but the damn
boards wouldn't drive it!

Apparently (I didn't see it) API announced a new board which had
ES40-like memory bandwidth and a 66 Mhz PCI bus late last year or
early this one, but the announcement was pulled off their Web page
almost immediately and they wouldn't answer questions.

And then you could ask about why the ES45 took so long to get from
first claimed delivery to full production and where the DS25 and
DS15 are.

If the project had been properly managed, the ES45, DS25 and DS15
with 1 GHz chips would have been in full production by January
2001.  I could tell you a lot of tenders that would have gone
Compaq's way, large and small, if that had happened.

All of the above has been published or stated publicly, so I am
not leaking anything.


