nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) wrote in 
news:9vvjf9$3u2$1@pegasus.csx.cam.ac.uk:
>|> nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) writes
>|> > Note that by "the EVx" I am also referring to chipsets and so on,
>|> > without which the chip is useless and cannot support faster clock 
>|> > speeds even on the same chip - witness the 1 GHz Alpha and ES45
>|> > shambles. 
> The DS10/DS20/ES40 boards were already inadequate for the 667 MHz
> Alphas, both because of memory bandwidth and because of their 33 MHz
> PCI bus.  The latter was particularly painful for HPC customers,
> because they could get a faster network (Quadrics) but the damn
> boards wouldn't drive it!

I think some of these points are pretty important and not being heard in 
this thread.

I've thought all along a big reason it was easy for CPQ to toss in the 
towel on Alpha was it would releave them of the burden of not just having 
to keep up with competing CPUs, but the burden of having to keep up with 
all the related technologies, such as memory and IO technologies.  

The evidence shows they're having a hard time doing this with the current 
generation (see Nick's points above, and also consider the IO technology in 
the first-generation Wildfires, etc) and continuing to do so across at 
least three different hardware design points (desktop/small server with 1-2 
CPUs, departmental server with 4-8 CPUs, enterprise server with 32-128 
CPUs) is incredibly expensive in terms of software and hardware 
development, testing, deployment and support.

And the decision was being made during the dot.com boom, and I personally 
know CPQ was having a hard time retaining employees, so there was lots of 
concern around having enough of the right kind of talent needed to keep the 
Alpha platform viable long term.  Now the world has changed in that regard, 
but it's hard to reverse time, isn't it?

By going to Itanium, you can just use Intel reference designs and CPQ 
Wintel products for the bottom two tiers of hardware, and concentrate on 
coming up with a box with competitive differentiation for the high-margin 
enterprise market. It's pretty clear Intel has been pretty good at getting 
best-in-class memory and IO technology into their products in the bottom 
two tiers.  It's also clear that the folks calling the shots at CPQ think 
they can come up with best-in-class products in the enterprise class.  Of 
course, that's open to some debate, which is what we all like about USENET.

I thought that strategy was solid right up till the CPQ/HP merger fiasco.  
Now it looks like all the UNIX business is fleeing, and I wonder if there's 
enough VMS business left to keep this business model afloat.  But, then 
again, if the world is still supporting the continuance of the 
Burroughs/Sperry/Univac product lines, it can be done.



