> I get the feeling you want to use the clone "As the Backup".  That is
> to say, all the previous replies said or suggested you should
>
>         clonefset
>         mount clone
>         vdump clone to backup media
>         umount clone
>         rmfset clone
>
> And while the vdump was happening the amount of extra space being used
> would be equal to the number of 8K pages modified in the original
> fileset.
>
> Since this didn't seem to answer your question, I am wondering if when
> you said "I have To clone File System To win Time On Backup", if maybe
> you were saying you could not afford the time to do the vdump and you
> wanted to know if you could just use a clone "As the Backup".
>
> Assuming this was the question, then there are problems with this
> approach when using AdvFS clones.  But to answer the question of how
> much disk space would you need.  You would need to have an equal amount
> of space to what you are currently using assuming you are going to keep
> the clone for an extended period of time and that over that period of
> time most of the files in the original fileset are going to be
> modified.
>
> As to problems.  You can only have 1 AdvFS clone per fileset at a time.
> So if you do not vdump the AdvFS clone to backup media, then to clone
> the fileset again, you will first need to remove the current clone.
> This would leave a gap in your backup strategy.  When you rmfset to
> remove the clone, AdvFS has to delete all the storage used by the
> clone.  The longer the AdvFS clone has existed, and the more data it
> has captured, the longer it will take to preform the rmfset, again
> widening the gap in the badkup strategy.  And while the clone is in
> effect, there is extra overhead when modifications are made to the
> original file while AdvFS allocates storage for the clone, copies the
> original data to clone, then make the modification to the original
> file.
>
> If you want to uses clones and ONLY clones as a backup strategy for
> speed reasons, then consider using LSM mirrors and splitting them or
> hardware storage system mirrors, clones, or snapshots.
>
> I hope this has answered your question.
>
>                                         Bob Harris




