"J. Shailim" wrote (in part):

> On a Tru64 4.0 F unix, i would like to know :
>
> 1)As the Enhanced Security Mode is not enabled only with the
> installation of the related packages (osfc2secXXX and osfxc2secXXX),
> how to know that the secsetup command has been launch ?

I don't think I really understand your question here, but here goes....

To see what security option you'll have on next system reboot:

    # /usr/sbin/rcmgr get SECURITY BASE

That will either display BASE or ENHANCED.

To see what security option your next attempted login will get (exclusive of
dtlogin
with CDE, which is the reason why secsetup recommends a reboot):

    # grep '^siad_ses_init=' /etc/sia/matrix.conf

That will show a line which will either contain BSD (for BASE security) or
OSFC2
(for ENHANCED security).


> 2)How to know wich options have been set during the secsetup
> procedure.

Not really, but ...
    # /tcb/bin/edauth -g -dd default
    # /sbin/sysconfig -q vm vm-segmentation
    # grep '^AUDIT' /etc/rc.config
    # dbx -k /vmunix
    (dbx) p paclpolicy
    (dbx) q

(That last will show a value of 0 if acls are ENABLED.)
(Also, that last can be replaced in V5.x with:
    # sysconfig -q sec acl_mode
)


> 3) is it normal that the authck -a command outputs nothing ?

Yes.  Without the -v option, authck only reports on errors it finds.

Hth.

