One or more blocks of the disk aren't readable.  It could be a sign
that the disk is going bad, or it could just be a small number of
blocks have become unreadable.  The first AdvFS give the block number
with respect to the beginning of the partition.  Usually, the C partition
is the whole disk, so that probably the real LBA.  The name of the device
(rrzf) suggests the data is on an array controller of some sort, which
can affect the repair.

>I execute "/sbin/advfs/tag2name /pj45_data/.tags/2" and the answare is
>"tag2name: statfs(/pj45_data/.tags/2) error; [5] I/O error"

This is what suggests there is more than one error.  Not only is there
an error in the original file, but there is an error in the tag data.

>
>if I go in the /pj45_data the system go in coredump.

This suggests file system corruption sufficiently extensive that the
system paniced.

>Someone can help me

Boot the system without touching the domain with the problem.  Check
the error log to see what the underlying errors really were.  It
could be a few bad blocks, a bad disk or something wrong with the
whole subsystem.  I don't see a hint in the information provided
to say what version your running or what the system is.  Older versions
can format the error log with uerf(8).  If you can use it, use the
option "-o full" to get the full event listing for the storage subsystem
errors.  DECevent, where available, does a better job of formatting
the event log.  There was a time when the format of the event log
was changing that some versions of it apparently couldn't read all
event logs.  I don't know whether later versions fixed this.  Compaq
Analyze is designed to do analysis instead of bare bit to text of
event log entries, but the "trans" option may some bit to text.

Recovery of the disk and data depends on the subsystem and its configuration.
Many SCSI disks support a command to replace bad blocks with good ones.
It is an optional command though and not all disks may have it.  Replacing
a block doesn't guarantee that the contents will be correct since that
depends on being able to read or regenerate a copy of the data.  If the
block was bad in the first place, then it is probably impossible to read
a good copy of data.  Tru64 UNIX will try to replace a bad block if it
can get a good copy of the data.  Many subsystems will to.

Some subsystems track the state of the data (the HSZ/HSG family) and
can replace the block without forgetting that the data was bad.  They
continue to present read errors for the data even though they have
replaced the bad block with a good one.  Rewriting the block with new
data will fix this state.

