In article <d31a72dc.0112170701.6f5b5f06@posting.google.com>, dherri@acxiom.com (Dave) writes:

>I have been given a system with 30 different RAID-5 devices and would
>like to benchmark the performance of them.  Most are 6 member sets,
>with a few 4 member sets.  The disks are 18.2 Universal WideUltra3 15K
>disks.  My thought was to place a 10GB file on each device, then one
>by one copy the file (using "cp") to /dev/null.
>
>Is this a valid way to test the performance of the devices?

The test is reasonably valid if your intended I/O is mostly reading
very large files from the particular file system you're using on
the device.  If the actual I/O load is going to be write intensive
or random I/Os in a mix, then the test isn't representitive of the
load.

As another follow-up suggested (that I saw) you can use dd(1) to
test the device level performance without a file system getting
in the way.  I don't think dd(1) does any multi-buffered I/O, so
a file system that does read-ahead actually has an advantage
doing lots of sequential reads.  If you plan to put a file system
on the device, then doing the tests through a file system is
reasonable, since it will affect the final performance you see.

>The
>reason I ask is that I'm consistently getting a wide varience in
>performance.  Devices a, b, and c are all 6 member RAID-5 sets with
>roughly the same free space, yet a and b yield 49MB/sec while c is
>34MB/sec.

Without knowing more about the subsystem or configuration it is hard
to guess what might be causing the problem.  There may be affects
due to the back-end configuration (are all the devices on different
back-end busses).  If the subsystem supports hardware partitioning
of a larger RAID, then different partitions could easily have different
performance.  Most (all?) high capacity disks are zoned; they have
different number of sectors per track depending on the track.  Tracks
with more data have the potention of offering high data rates.

>
>Any thoughts?
