In article <H1tU7.68658$Zd.6364033@bin1.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com>,
Bill Todd <billtodd@metrocast.net> wrote:
>
> [ HEAVILY snipped ]
>
>Whatever Itanic will come after Madison is far more likely to be an EPIC
>architecture with Alpha-inspired tweaks than an Alpha architecture running
>the Itanic instruction set.
>
>One of Alpha's strengths was its instruction set, which was explicitly
>designed to allow performance enhancements.  The design behind Itanic's
>instruction set was based on completely different (EPIC) assumptions which
>are less amenable to using Alpha-style performance approaches even if the
>entire EPIC implementation is abandoned.
>
>It is possible that some of the on-chip memory and multi-processor support
>could be grafted onto the McKinley/Madison core by 2005, and there's some
>indication that this may be what you are referring to as 'Itanium 2' (i.e.,
>a processor core with absolutely no Alpha technology in it, and hence just
>as much a pig as McKinley/Madison will be).  And while Compaq has been
>promoting the fiction that it might appear in 2004, again this does not seem
>to be consistent with private information from the transplanted Alpha team.

There is one, extreme, approach to this supersonic swine that Intel
may be taking.  That of converting the EPIC instruction set into
Alpha-like RISC micro-operations and have an EV8-like engine execute
those.  While I can believe that this could be made to work, I cannot
see that it could be made to fly.

The whole point of the EPIC design was that compilers could use it
to produce fantastically fast code - and, as some of us said in the
very beginning would happen, this has not proved possible except for
a subset of HPC codes.  I cannot see that an EV8 engine executing
EPIC micro-operations is going to be worth the effort of designing.

However, one really interesting question about this (and the dates
of 2005/6, which I believe) is what happened to the original
Madison and Deerfield?

Back in 1994, the plan was that Madison and Deerfield would be a
new design (perhaps two), and that clearly has been cancelled so
thoroughly that it cannot be resuscitated.  To save face, the code
names have been applied to McKinley process shrinks and tweaks.
Not Intel's traditional practice.

Furthermore, it means that the IA-64 line is currently scheduled
to have a 4+ year gap between designs, which is further evidence
of a cancelled design project.  So why was it cancelled?


