nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) writes:

>Back in 1994, the plan was that Madison and Deerfield would be a
>new design (perhaps two), and that clearly has been cancelled so
>thoroughly that it cannot be resuscitated.  To save face, the code
>names have been applied to McKinley process shrinks and tweaks.
>Not Intel's traditional practice.


Well of course Intel's not talking (what else is new?) but if you believe
the stories coming from HP people the last few years, the way it worked
was that Intel engineers pretty much did the Merced design themselves
with the help of a few HP guys, and HP pretty much did McKinley themselves
with a few Intel guys.  Madison/Deerfield was a single design (with a low
and high end implementation) done by Intel, as sort of a Merced II.  Well,
given all the problems Merced ended up with, and the improvements in
McKinley, a McKinley shrink was projected to easily beat Merced II, so it
was dropped, and the McKinley shrink took over for it.

I suppose all the Alpha guys (not to mention the HP guys) that have been
brought in by Intel over the past year are to make sure that the follow on
clean slate design by Intel (let's call it Merced III) doesn't suck so
much.  Until that bears fruit, Itanium may be in the same boat as PA-RISC,
relying on a core that is repeatedly shrunk and tweaked :)

That might also explain why McKinley is coming out in .18u, despite its
market debut coming about a year after Intel's first .13u product.  If
they do a .13u McKinley today they don't have anything they can call
"Madison", and people start to wonder what is going on with their roadmap.

