"Bill Todd" <billtodd@metrocast.net> writes:

[sorry for the large context required]
> > What happened was that the EV8 development team went to Capellas and
> > said that without a lot more funding it would be impossible to keep up
> > with the X86 class of processors (and possibly Itanium ?)
> 
> I very much doubt that any such mission to Capellas occurred - it sounds
> much more like the spin Compaq tried to palm off as part of the June 25th
> announcement that the decision had started with the Alpha engineers.  They
> later amended this to saying that it originated with some of the *server*
> (not processor) designers - and there's an interesting political tension
> between the two groups involving the migration of much of what had been
> server 'turf' onto the chip in EV7.

Agreed with the last sentence - hadn't thought about it this way. But as you
say, this existed since the first design for the EV7 was made.

> There was *no question whatsoever* in the minds of the EV8 team that they
> would extend, not simply maintain, Alpha's current performance advantage
> over Itanic.

And that's the point your correspondant _wasn't_ addressing: it explicitly was
the comparison to x86 processors, and not IA64 processors.

> Keeping ahead of IA32 processors would have been more difficult but far from
> impossible, at least for Intel variants (Hammer might be harder to match):

See above.

> If IA32 were truly Alpha's competition, that argument might have at least
> some merit.  But if a 32-bit architecture were considered sufficient in that
> market, there would have been absolutely no reason for Intel to have
> developed a 64-bit machine that shows no evidence that it will *ever* come
> even close to catching up with IA32 on simple speed benchmarks [...]

Quite. 

What makes the postulated meeting less credible is the argument that
comparison was with x86 processors, because that's not where Alpha is
currently competing, and it's not where it will compete in future.

On the other hand, I can imagine those guys telling Cappelas they were
not getting enough money to follow through with their roadmap as planned.
One might see the initial delay in delivering the 21264, and the very much
lengthened development of the tweaked processors (as somebody said, we would
have expected the 21264-1001 18 months ago, not now), as evidence for
sufficient development funds. So if they went to Cappelas and said, "hey,
either give us x% more funding, or it's not worth bothering to do it because
others will take this market", that's plausible. Capellas reaction of then
axing Alpha development is also plausible, if irrational as a business
decision (for all the arguments you make).

	Jan


