> root       2000 99.5  0.1 6.41M 112K ??       R      Dec 13
>  2-06:09:27 -ACBC7716.ipt.a (ftpd)

Bad thing.

> I checked various log files but didn't find anything particularly
> interesting.  Compaq's website wasn't very helpful either.  However I did
> figure out that "ACBC7716.ipt.a" is an AOL host name
"acbc7716.ipt.aol.com".
> As of this writing, that host is still pingable, but there was no trace of
> login from the host in the log files.

You can certainly do two things:

1. run "ftpd" in Debug mode.

Change definition in /etc/inetd.conf of ftpd to contain:

ftp     stream  tcp     nowait  root    /usr/sbin/ftpd          ftpd -dl

This will log everything to /var/adm/syslog.dated/<DATE>/daemon.log

2. Run tcpdump and sniff for that session.

In order to configure tcpdump the following must be done:

2.1. reconfigure kernel to contain PACKETFILTER module
2.2. (OPTIONAL) bring the desired interface in promiscuous mode "ifconfig
tu0 +promisc"
2.3. configure packetfilter module on that interface "pfconfig -i tu0 +c +p"
("+p" is optional)

Then try to see if there is any traffic on that connection (I bet there
isn't):

tcpdump -i tu0 `host acbc7716.ipt.aol.com`

Nix.




