"Adam Price" <adam+usenet@pappnase.co.uk> wrote in message 
> No this is expected behaviour. The ping send out 1 packet to the broadcast
> address. It then receives packets back from lots of machines, the first one
> to respond is usually itself, meaning all of the others are duplicates since
> they are in response to a request which has already been answered.
> How do the HP-UX boxes behave?
> Adam Price

Thanks for your reply, Adam!
Do you mean that it is _normal_ behaviour of ping in Tru64 and you get
the same result on your system?

64 bytes from 1.2.3.5: icmp_seq=1 ttl=64 time=0 ms
64 bytes from 1.2.3.1: icmp_seq=1 ttl=255 time=0 ms (DUP!)
64 bytes from 1.2.3.2: icmp_seq=1 ttl=255 time=0 ms (DUP!)
64 bytes from 1.2.3.3: icmp_seq=1 ttl=255 time=0 ms (DUP!)

I guess it could also mean that each packet is received twice (e.g.,
two ICMP packets with icmp_seq=1 from 1.2.3.1 and so on). 1.2.3.5 is
the calling system. HP-UX boxes receive broadcast ping replies without
(DUP!) mark.

Manual for Tru64 ping tells that 
'duplicate packets should never occur, and seem to be caused by
inappropriate link  level retransmissions.  Duplicates may occur in
many situations and are
rarely (if ever) a good sign...'. So, I'm really worried about it. It
would be nice if there is difference in ping implementation in
different UNIX products.

BR,
Dub


