Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> 
> jmfbahciv@aol.com writes:
> 
> > >They didn't "buy Alpha".  They wanted the services businees of Digital,
> > >but Alpha was a mandatory part of the deal.
> > Do you know why it was mandatory?  I never understood that part.
> 
> Would the DEC BOD and shareholders been so stupid to allow Palmer to sell only
> DEC Services to Compaq? Neither part could have survived for long.
> 
>         Jan

Is the service business necessary to the customer these days or is it
necessary to the vendor?  My impression of IBM (from the RISC-6000 line)
is that they engineered in a lot of features to make the customer
dependent on IBM service. 

My site moved from an 8650 through a Vax 4000 to a Vaxstation 4000. 
There's still a service contract on the Vaxstation (maybe this is the
last year), but that's because many parts are single-source (at this
point you could probably buy at least 2 or 3 usd 4000/90's on Ebay for
the annual maintenance cost (which isn't that high).  With the 8650 I
saw the CE at least twice a year (PM).  With the 4000/90 I wasn't even
sure if he was still employed (Compaq did let a bunch of servic people
go after they bought DEC.)

Which leads to the question of whether Digital minus it's service arm
could still be a successful computer vendor, so that Compaq could have
spun off Alpha, VMS, and Digital Unix fairly easily.  Still could, if
they hadn't gutted Alpha so badly.

Is the main problem with this the possibiity that whoever bought Digital
could make a big success of it, thereby revealing the error involved in
spinning it off in the first place?  There's also the VMS and Digital
Unix revenue, which isn't peanuts, so that could be another reason to
keep the product.  But Compaq has a strange notion of how to manage a
cash cow if that's the case.

Mainframe computer companies did get fat and lazy, so that those with a
PC frame of mind could rightly see that customers were paying far more
for functionality than was justified.  I'd think it would make more
sense to slim down traditional OS's (e.g., VMS) than to try to beef
Windows up to take their place.  Windows is a fat target for somebody
who wants to sell to customers who desire simplicity and reliability. 
Microsoft will probably first expand Windows in a crude way to make it
look like it has the power of a VMS, then spend years re-engineering and
solving the problems Microsoft always seems to engineer in to a
product.  (I suspect the gist of what I've just said has already been
discussed to death here.  Please forgive the ignorant repeat of what you
already know.)


