Those who think Compaq is mindlessly in bed with Microsoft should take note
of Capellas's keynote at Oracle OpenWorld.  Based on my experience over the
last 2 years with CETS planning it appears to that Compaq has adopted a
triangulation policy with Microsoft and Oracle.

There are very solid business reasons why both Compaq would want to
triangulate Microsoft with Oracle and why Intel would want to triangulate
Microsoft with HP/Compaq non-Microsoft OS's.  Andy Grove has tried to
diversify the IA dependence on Microsoft before but he could not reach
critical mass.  It is something he dearly would love to do, has publicly
stated so, and the opportunity now exists if the HP/Compaq merger happens.
The straw that appears to have broken the back with Capellas and Microsoft
was Microsoft's failure to give Compaq credit when Microsoft beat the
competition on a TP benchmark using Proliants that Compaq had helped
Microsoft tune.  Rumor has it  a high level decision was made to play the
Oracle card.

The interesting thing about the article, and the blind faith assumption that
some in this newsgroup have, is the assumption as fact that Microsoft
controls Compaq.  That blind faith assumption ignores that even an idiot CEO
would see that pursuing that path means it would place Compaq in a situation
where its market differentiator was price since everyone would have the same
product.  That is a bad place to be.  A classic example of that type of
market is the airline industry - Boeing (and maybe someday Airbus) make all
the real profits and the airlines fighting it out over price.  Southwest
being the Dell example today and Continental being what Compaq hopes to
become.

It just never ceases to amaze me when I read articles like the one below
that people discount Compaq and Intel acting in their own self interest by
making sure they control as much of their product technology as possible.
To that end the name of the game is to triangulate Microsoft and not becomes
its slave...


