Hi Tobias,

Your point about the bias of familiarity is well-taken, and I do try to
compensate for this when comparing different versions of Unix.

I am a Linux geek in the sense that it's my favorite version of Unix.  My
exposure is broader, though; in the last 15 years I've professionally used or
administered SunOS, AIX, Ultrix, OSF1, IRIX, Solaris, HP-UX, BSD, SCO, System5
(3B2) as well as Linux.

> All in all, bot systems have their strengths and weeknesses. For example, I
> have yet to see anything as easy-to-use and as reliable as AdvFS for Linux.

Funny you should mention AdvFS.  For me, filesystem reliability is a point
that Linux wins hands-down.  I've recently had 'verify' (AdvFS's fsck)
completely trash a filesystem that was only slightly inconsistent (a couple of
bad directory entries) at the user level.  I've offered Compaq an image that
trips the bug, but it doesn't seem like they're eager to track this down.  (If
anyone knows the name of the person at Compaq whose burning desire in life is
to make sure that AdvFS is utterly reliable, please have them contact me.)

> Likewise, I would love to see something like a rpm/dpkg system for GNU 
> software on Tru64.

Me, too.  Debian's 'apt-get' provides easy access to high-quality, up-to-date
builds of virtually every software package I know of.  I can install it all in
a few minutes.

> In my opinion, Tru64 is very easy to use and easiy to maintain with the one
> exception of having to compile all that GNU stuff by hand, which is a real
> pain.

It sure is.  (Especially since several key tools seem not to have been ported
to Tru64 5.1, but that's another story.)

> But you do that once for your whole suite of Tru64 machines and then export
> /usr/local via NFS, and from then on it doesn't bother you any more.

Yes, except that you have to do it all over again when new versions are
released.  Linux (esp Debian) definitely wins here.


To me, it looks like all commercial Unixes (Tru64 included) are a win right
now only for very limited problem domains, usually where larger scale than
Linux can provide is required in a single box.  That niche shrinks every day.

Mike


