> Fred Kleinsorge wrote:
> > TURNING.  GET OVER IT.  Nothing says Compaq has to continue building Alpha
> > systems until the end of time.  Nothing says that it is a requirement that
> > Compaq build something faster than Intel.
> 
> But you're not seing it the way customers are seing it. The way we see it,
[snip]

The way "we" see it? Uh-oh, it's the "we" syndrome -- attempting to
strengthen one's personal opinions by pretending to speak for a mass
audience.

Let me tell you how this customer sees it.

I've got business needs. Is my current Alpha environment meeting any
of my business needs today? Yes. Has the Alpha series served me well
over the years? Yes.

Have I ever had to replace an Alpha because of shifting business
needs? You bet. That first Alpha I used all those years ago (and the
software that ran on it at the time) would NOT meet my needs today.

Moreover, every computer system will need to be replaced sooner or
later as my business needs grow or shift. My tasks are to pick the
right time, and to do the transition effectively.

In essence, there's a pair of questions I want to be able to answer at
any time regarding any computing system I'm using:
1) Is it meeting my business needs effectively today?
2) Based on my best guesses about upcoming trends in my business needs
vs. upcoming trends regarding this computing system, when will its
ability to meet my business needs diminish?

Even when Compaq was touting a long design future for the Alpha, I
still had to ask myself those questions. Questions like continued ISV
support for OpenVMS Alpha loomed large. Questions like the Alpha's
continued commercial viability over the years crossed my mind. It
would have been irresponsible NOT to consider those questions. I had
my concerns, but nothing indicated to me that the bottom was about to
fall out.

Currently, I still have an Alpha that's meeting my business needs
pretty well. I have one critically important application that's
becoming a problem child, however, because the ISV is bailing out from
the OpenVMS platform (and from reality overall, but that's a comic
tale I'll save for another day). However, I have other uses that
continue to justify my Alpha, so I'm about to buy another one while we
leave the problem child around as a legacy system until we can find a
new way to fulfill that particular business need.

So, I still wonder about things like continued ISV support and
continued commercial viability. I see that many ISVs have hopped onto
the OpenVMS/IPF bandwagon. In particular, there are two that I _need_
that have stated their support for OpenVMS on IPF. If it weren't for
them, I probably wouldn't be buying another Alpha right now.

Have I ever been disappointed with Compaq's messages, or lack thereof?
Sure, but I've also gotten some OUTSTANDING support from Compaq in
various ways, and I'm not going to throw that away lightly.

In other words, I have no reason to try to sabotage the Alpha/IPF
effort by proclaiming ulterior motives, secret agendas, or massive
incompetence. It's in my best interests to see this Alpha/IPF decision
become as successful as it can. I've seen no indications that the
decision might be reversed, and nothing but speculation and wishful
thinking that a reversal would be best in the long run.

--
