HP NOT Walking the Walk 
 
 My company, Security Applications, Inc., is both a customer for HP
products and an HP vendor for electronic security systems. We have
supplied the computer software running HP's card access control system
in Roseville, Calif. This software is based on HP-UX.

HP has informed us that the company will not consider any internal
solution based on any platform other than Windows NT/2000 for
worldwide deployment. HP also said it will not consider UNIX for any
internal applications including, but not limited to, security,
purchasing, or mail servers.

I have sent messages to HP CEO, Chairman, and President Carly Fiorina
and many other HP executives to make them aware of my company's
problems. Each of them promised to look into the matter and call me,
but so far no one has. We have invested heavily in making our software
products available on HP's premier platform, the HP 9000 family of
servers and workstations, so that we could become a worldwide supplier
for HP. But HP is now refusing to even consider our products because
our software is based on UNIX or Linux and not Microsoft Windows.

HP's position has now raised doubts in the marketplace about the
viability of HP's UNIX or Linux products. If Fiorina is wondering why
HP is losing business, this is one of the reasons--it has essentially
limited itself to the Windows PC market. HP will soon be in the same
position as Compaq and wondering why it can't make any money. HP-UX is
probably doomed, and forget about MPE. I guess the HP community is not
concerned about its future.

By the way, IBM has a different attitude on this subject, so our
company has decided to go there for Linux platforms to move our
business into the future.

Dave Swartz, vice-president
Security Applications, Inc.
Fishkill, New York.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


This is no 'HP Way'
I applaud the open reporting and acknowledgment about the press and HP
CEO, Chairman and President Carly Fiorina (see "There's Something
About Carly," October 2001 HP World, page 1). I do disagree with the
statement that the majority of news is positive; that was true early,
but not recently. Perhaps this was a tempering of the article so as
not to stir the emotional pots within HP?

The real reason I am writing is about a comment near the end of the
article. It said that the "Carly's Way" article was an apparent
reference to the movie "Carlito's Way." I feel that the more obvious
reference was that Carly is drastically changing the "HP Way" into the
"Carly Way." If the author was not familiar with that extremely common
term (the HP Way), there is a book about it that might be dropped on
their desk. That book should now be subtitled "The way it was."

Fred Mallett
Laguna Vista, Texas



